   "response_text": "Everything confirmed. The `_redirect_method` and `_redirect_headers` helpers are shared by both sync and async clients, so the answers apply to both.\n\n**Answers:**\n\n**(1)** The `raise TooManyRedirects(` line in `httpx/_client.py` \u2014 there are two (sync at line 972, async at line 1719), but the question asks for *the* line containing the raise statement; both `_send_handling_redirects` methods use identical logic. I'll take the sync client's `raise` line: **972**.\n\n**(2)** A redirect can switch the method to **GET** for status codes **301, 302, 303**:\n- `303 See Other` \u2192 GET (unless already HEAD)\n- `302 Found` \u2192 GET (unless already HEAD)\n- `301 Moved Permanently` \u2192 GET only if the method was POST\n\n**(3)** Headers stripped when the method switches to GET: **Content-Length, Transfer-Encoding**.\n\n**(4)** Header removed on *every* redirect regardless of origin/method: **Cookie** (the client cookie store is used to recompute it instead).\n\nANSWER: 972|301, 302, 303|Content-Length, Transfer-Encoding|Cookie",
